hi my name is Chelsea and I'm an MIT  sophomore studying computer science and  molecular biology and I'm here to  explain to you how websites are built so  when we're looking at a website that  you're trying to visit there are two  computers we care about there's you  sometimes called the client side and  there's them the server side the website  you're trying to access so when you're  browsing the Internet you type in a URL  like facebook.com/ and you send a  request to a computer that Facebook owns  once it gets there Facebook has a bunch  of things that it needs to do first of  all it has a database a database is kind  of like a bookshelf containing books  about all of the users of Facebook so  they'll have a book about you it's got  information about your friends and your  posts your likes and your comments maybe  it has a picture of you and all of this  is stored in the database  next the information from the database  goes through the website code which is  what all of the programmers at Facebook  pour all of their effort and time into  making and the website code generates  something called an HTML file  hTML is a bunch of code in another  language which describes exactly how the  layout of the website is going to be  once it reaches you so now that they  have this file containing all of the  HTML the JavaScript etc it's passed back  through the internet and arrives at you  we're a software program called the  browser which you're already using to  explore the web we'll take the HTML file  and render it into something that looks  pretty such as your Facebook homepage as  it was meant to be and once it's there  you can click view source from the  browser and see what the original HTML  was meant to look like so yeah that's  how websites are built from them all the  way up to you
 

